  "text": "Solution 2 is wrong, and Solution 1's answer of 3/7 is consistent with everything I can check.\n\n**Solution 2's lower bound fails.** Its claimed extremal example gives only 2/5, but the $\\mathbb{Z}_7$ circulant from Solution 1 beats it. Take arcs $i\\to i+1$ and $i\\to i+3$ on $\\mathbb{Z}_7$:\n- The generators $\\{1,3\\}$ and their negatives $\\{4,6\\}$ are disjoint, so this is an oriented graph.\n- Each vertex has out-degree 2 and in-degree 2, so $\\Delta = 4$.\n- The directed triangles correspond to step sequences summing to $0 \\bmod 7$ with steps in $\\{1,3\\}$. The only such multiset is $\\{1,3,3\\}$, giving exactly 7 triangles, with each vertex in 3 of them.\n- Two vertices hit at most 6 triangles, so $fvs \\ge 3$.\n- Deleting $\\{0,2,4\\}$ leaves $\\{1,3,5\\}$ with the single arc $5\\to1$, which is acyclic. So $fvs = 3$.\n\nHence $c^* \\ge 3/7 > 2/5$, and Solution 2's answer cannot be right. Disjoint copies of this graph give ratio exactly $3/7$.\n\n**Solution 1's upper bound is not verified.** Its argument that $fvs \\le \\lfloor 3n/7\\rfloor$ for all $\\Delta\\le 4$ is only sketched, and I did not check it. My recollection is that $3n/7$ is the known tight bound for this class, but I'm not certain of that. The answer 3/7 is at least consistent with the lower bound I verified.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
